## Choose an environment

Lappet is the planned native runtime and Rust host-binding library for Moth. It
lives beside the compiler in the same repository. Its first consumers are Rust
game engines and server applications. Browser programmes continue to use the
HTML builder and browser execution.

The initial crate contains design documents and a roadmap only. Runtime
execution, native Core implementations and package installation are not yet
available through it. These pages describe the agreed direction without adding
new working commands.

### Use an official builder

Official builders are distributed with the Moth CLI and their required
libraries. The intended application-author workflow needs only that distribution
on a supported platform. It does not require installing Rust, Cargo, Node.js or
a separate Wasm runtime. Developing Moth or writing a custom Rust host has its
own tooling requirements.

All user-facing commands keep the `moth` name. Lappet identifies the library and
runtime implementation, not another executable to learn. Future command syntax
will be documented when its own design and implementation are complete.

### Embed Moth in Rust

A Rust application can use the compiler to build a Moth project and Lappet to
execute its Wasm. The builder selects host packages, owns application state and
chooses when exported Moth functions run. Lappet handles the runtime and typed
binding boundary rather than a game loop or a server framework.

For example, a game engine can expose a checked entity API while keeping entity
storage in Rust. A server can expose its own services without granting every
programme ambient filesystem or network access. V1 calls are synchronous.

Rust host functions may use ordinary Rust libraries or wrap C libraries. These
are trusted native dependencies, outside the Wasm sandbox. Their safety and
blocking behaviour remain the host author's responsibility. The package manager
will not install native code merely because a Moth package asks for it.

## Keep Core consistent

Official native and browser environments share compiler-owned Core signatures
and semantics. Each implementation preserves documented host differences and
capability requirements. A headless server does not pretend to have a window
just to make a title operation succeed.

Custom builders can use selected Core implementations or provide none. Core
names, including the `io` prelude, stay reserved. A custom builder cannot replace
their meaning or gain official certification simply by implementing them.

The future official package manager admits Core-dependent packages only for
officially supported environments. Custom builders receive no-core packages
only, even when they use Lappet. Local and builder-supplied code can still use
the APIs that their host actually supports.

No-core means no direct or transitive dependency on public Core APIs. It does
not remove the runtime requirements of ordinary language features such as
collections. Every target still validates the Moth features it can execute.

## Understand the execution policy

Lappet V1 uses Wasmtime, with Winch for development and Cranelift for release by
default. A builder can override that selection. Moth's output profile and the
native compilation strategy remain separate choices with the same language
semantics.

Persistent AOT caches, async scheduling, foreign Wasm components and automatic
native dependency installation are later work. JavaScript bindings remain a
browser-builder capability and are not available in Lappet. File-backed WAT
also requires an explicit provider and supported ABI rather than a general
foreign-package loader.

## Add dependencies deliberately

The planned package manager favours small, inspectable dependency graphs. Its
first managed packages contain Moth source and are classified as no-core or
Core-dependent. Dependencies on specialised Builder APIs remain outside that
initial managed ecosystem.

An addition begins with dependency metadata checks, then obtains source in
isolated staging for assessment. The report distinguishes dependency depth,
breadth and size from security findings. A shallow dependency graph is not
proof that a package is safe.

Failed checks or insufficient evidence cause rejection. Only an admissible
proposal reaches user confirmation, and the project changes only after that
confirmation. Downloading source for inspection is not installing a dependency.
Exact assessment tools and limits remain future design checkpoints.

## Follow the design

- @../../developer-docs/project-builders (Rust project builders) explains the
  compiler SDK and host-binding contracts.
- @../../developer-docs/wasm-optimisation (Wasm optimisation) owns the shared
  compiler and native-code optimisation policy.
- @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/blob/main/crates/lappet/docs/design.md (Runtime
